 "post": {
  "title": "Why Overthinking Makes You Miss #shorts",
  "description": "Last hole of mini golf. Dev, the gym coach, overthinks a two-foot putt: grip, elbows, breathe. And misses. It isn't nerves alone, it's attention. In 2002, Sian Beilock, Thomas Carr, Clare MacMahon and Janet Starkes (Journal of Experimental Psychology: Applied) had experienced golfers putt under two conditions: one that made them attend to their step-by-step putting, and one with a second task that pulled attention away. They putted more accurately in the distracting dual-task condition. Novices were the opposite: they did better when focusing on each step. Their idea (explicit monitoring): watching a well-learned skill step by step can disrupt it, one of the ways people choke under pressure (see also Beilock & Carr, 2001). Comment 'got me' if you've ever forgotten how to walk normally. #psychology #shorts #overthinking #choking #sportspsychology",
  "tags": [
   "choking under pressure",
   "overthinking",
   "sports psychology",
   "golf",
   "psychology",
   "Beilock"
  ]
